Feature: Runtime detection with Falco
  Falco watches the system calls of every process on every node and names what a workload should not do: a shell spawned in a pod, a write under /etc, a read of a service account token by something that should not. It runs on every node with the least privilege its driver needs, with the rules its image ships, which nothing replaces at run time, and the exceptions the tree states, each with its reason and as narrow as the workload it is for; it asks for the memory its buffers and its own state take, and mounts no service account token, as nothing in it calls the API; its events are JSON lines the log collector already ships, its own metrics are scraped, and an alert fires when a rule of priority Critical or above has matched. It detects and never responds: no pod is killed or changed, and its events stay in the cluster.

  Four scenarios need a docker daemon, which the CI containers do not have: "Falco loads the local rules at the pinned version and compiles the redirect rule with its exceptions under a single negation", "Falco compiles the drop-and-execute rule with the one exception for Cilium's sysctl fix under a single negation", "Falco compiles the API server, shell and log-clearing rules with the tree's exceptions under a single negation each" and "A command without a terminal raises a named rule of the image, and a query finds its event". Their proofs run Falco itself, from the image the render names, by its digest, in a container without network, without a capability, with a read-only root and a user that is not root, on the render's configuration and local rules, and read what it prints: a dry run, the validation of the rules files and its listing of rules. No probe is loaded and no event is read. They run with the other proofs of tests/falco.bats wherever a docker daemon answers, on a developer's machine or an agent's, and skip, saying why, where none does, as under just ci and in CI, which run every other proof. No proof evaluates an event against a rule: that takes a capture a probe records on a running kernel, which would hold that machine's processes, users and addresses, so what a rule matches rests on the condition Falco compiles, and the values Falco reports for a workload's fields are seen only in an event on a node.

  Scenario: Falco is selected by overlay entries and builds nothing when it is not
    Given the lab-sto2 overlay, which selects Falco with the namespace directory security-system and Falco's ks.yaml
    When it is built with both entries, without Falco's ks.yaml and with no entry of security-system, and again with the monitoring definitions it needs left out
    Then with both it applies the namespace security-system, its default deny and the Kustomization falco, which waits and names prometheus-operator-crds and cilium; without Falco's ks.yaml it applies the namespace, its default deny and the namespace's other application, the flow recorder, and nothing of Falco; with no entry of security-system, nothing of security-system; and with the definitions left out it is refused, naming falco and the missing application

  Scenario: Falco runs on every node with the modern eBPF probe and only the privileges it names
    Given the falco application built as Flux applies it and its pinned chart rendered with the repository's values
    When the DaemonSet, its Pod Security level and its entry in the exceptions list are read
    Then one pod runs on every node, control planes included, with the modern eBPF probe, no kernel module and no init container, privileged is not set, and the container adds the capabilities BPF, PERFMON, SYS_PTRACE and SYS_RESOURCE and no others, mounts of the host only its probe, its container metadata and the user and group names of its events need, every mount naming a volume of the pod, and no host path volume that nothing mounts, and an empty pod security context with no annotation that relaxes a profile; the namespace enforces privileged and audits and warns at restricted; and the exceptions list holds the DaemonSet with the level privileged and a reason that names each capability and host path the baseline level refuses in it

  Scenario: Falco fetches no rule, plugin or driver at run time that the tree did not pin
    Given the falco application built as Flux applies it and its pinned chart rendered with the repository's values
    When the pod's containers, the chart's values and Falco's configuration are read
    Then falcoctl neither installs nor follows an artifact and is in no container, the rules and the container plugin are the files of the Falco image, which is named by its version and digest, a version Renovate moves with the chart, no integration that fetches or sends anything is on, and what Falco reads is only the rules files of its image and the one place for local rules

  Scenario: Falco's events are JSON lines on standard output that the log collector ships
    Given Falco's rendered configuration and the log collector's rendered configuration
    When the outputs of Falco and the path of a pod's log lines through the collector are read
