  Scenario: Falco's local rules have one place, which holds only what is true today
    Given the falco application's values and its pinned chart rendered with them
    When the key that carries local rule files, the ConfigMap the chart makes of it and the pod's mount of it are read
    Then the key holds one file, exceptions.yaml, of fourteen items, in this order, each of which overrides the condition of one placeholder macro of the image's rules, the first for a placeholder replacing it with one conjunction in parentheses and each further one appending or and one conjunction to it: the redirect rule's, the drop-and-execute rule's, the redirect rule's three times more, the API server rule's seven times, the shell rule's and the log-clearing rule's; each has its own reason in a block of comments directly above it, which says what its workload does that the rule matches and why, or that the cause is not established, and what still matches: the first's what httpd does and that a shell in that container still matches, the second's naming cilium-sysctlfix and saying how it runs, why Talos makes that match, that it names a path and not a file, so it excepts whatever that container runs at that one path, and that any binary the container runs from an upper layer at any other path, cilium-sysctlfix included, still matches, and each further one's the words the proof holds it to, the job pods that still match and, in each item that excepts in the namespace ascender, the price of a pod made to look like its container there among them; and there is no rule, list or other item; the chart makes the ConfigMap falco-rules of that file as the values hold it; the pod mounts it whole at /etc/falco/rules.d, which Falco's configuration lists after the image's rules and Falco watches for changes; under /etc/falco the pod mounts nothing else but its configuration file and an empty config.d, the one place the configuration names for further configuration; and the docs show every item's condition

